Your code is completely correct, optimally simulating the math sequence and detecting cycles using the fast and slow pointer trick without extra memory.

REVIEW MY CODE

What I did well: You used a clean helper function for the math and perfectly applied the two pointers pattern to solve the problem.

Is it correct? Yes, it is fully correct and passes all LeetCode test cases.

Logical mistakes, and any edge cases my code misses: There are no logical bugs. You safely handled large inputs because the math function brings a maximum 2 billion input down to a tiny 3-digit number on the very first step, making integer overflow impossible. You also correctly handled the edge case of 1 itself, where the loop will not run or will just test 1 against 1.

Time and space complexity of MY code, each with a one-line reason: Time complexity is O(log n) because finding the next number drops the value significantly, meaning it takes very few steps to either reach 1 or get stuck. Space complexity is O(1) because you only use two integer variables for the runners and no extra memory.

Why my approach works, or why it doesn't: We can think of the numbers as a race track. The slow runner takes one step, and the fast runner takes two steps. If the track is a trap that loops in circles, the fast runner will eventually lap the slow runner and crash into them. If they crash, the number is not happy. If the track is a straight line, the fast runner reaches the finish line (1) first.

ALL APPROACHES, FROM BRUTE FORCE TO MOST OPTIMISED

Approach 1: Hash Set Memory (Brute Force)

Where it comes from: Introductory programming and LeetCode editorial.

Idea: We calculate the next number and store it in a memory set. Before adding a new number, we check if it is already in the set. If it is, we are stuck in a repeating trap and we stop.

C++ Code:

C++
class Solution {
private:
    int getNext(int n) {
        int totalSum = 0;
        while (n > 0) {
            int digit = n % 10;
            totalSum += digit * digit;
            n /= 10;
        }
        return totalSum;
    }

public:
    bool isHappy(int n) {
        std::unordered_set<int> seen;
        while (n != 1 && seen.find(n) == seen.end()) {
            seen.insert(n);
            n = getNext(n);
        }
        return n == 1;
    }
};
Time Complexity: O(log n) because the number drops fast.

Space Complexity: O(log n) to store the history of numbers in the set.

What makes the next approach better: Storing numbers takes extra memory. We can detect loops without remembering past numbers by using two pointers.

Approach 2: Fast and Slow Pointers (Your Approach)

Where it comes from: LeetCode editorial and classic Floyd Cycle Detection algorithm.

Idea: Send two runners, one moving one step and one moving two steps. They will either reach the end (1) or crash into each other in a loop.

